The difference between interpolation and approximation (problem, solution, advantages, disadvantages)?

Problem: Interpolation requires each value to be on the corresponding function;
Approximation allows expressing the trend of the point distribution near the function.

Solution:
Interpolation: Lagrange interpolation method;
Approximation: Least squares method.

Advantages:
Interpolation: can accurately express the functional relationship of known values;
Approximation: fits real-life situations where data has deviations.

Disadvantages:
Interpolation: the resulting function may overfit and not match reality;
Approximation: the resulting function depends greatly on the number of samples.

① Interpolation: given a set of known data points, the interpolation function must strictly equal the original data;
Approximation: is a function that is as close as possible to these points overall.

② Interpolation emphasizes passing through every data point exactly, while approximation focuses on the overall trend;
interpolation error is zero but may lead to high-degree polynomials,
approximation has small overall error.

③ Advantages:
Interpolation is good at exactly passing through data points;
Disadvantages:
when the data size is large, results may be unstable, and complexity is high, requiring many equations to solve.

④ Advantages:
approximation can be more efficient and concise;
Disadvantages:
cannot exactly cover each data point.

⑤ Solution:
Approximation: optimization problems such as least squares, Chebyshev approximation?..;
Interpolation: solving linear systems, such as Lagrange evaluation.

Interpolation: interpolation algorithms must pass through the sample points. Because interpolation has to go through all sample points, it is prone to oscillation and can produce invalid results.
Advantages: interpolation is more precise because it passes through all sample points;
Disadvantages: more difficult to compute.
Solution: polynomial.

Approximation: approximation algorithms may not pass through the sample points;
Solution: least squares method.

Interpolation is a method of function fitting through polynomial interpolation.

Approximation is a method of function fitting through numerical approximation.

Advantages of interpolation: can perform function fitting more precisely;
Disadvantages: not intuitive enough.

Advantages of approximation: more intuitive;
Disadvantages: not precise enough.

Interpolation requires that the final fitting function strictly passes through all sample points, whereas approximation only requires being sufficiently close.

Advantages of interpolation: can fit the sample points to the maximum extent;
Disadvantages: easily distorted by extreme samples and noise, leading to overfitting.

Advantages of approximation: fits the samples to a certain extent and is less affected by noise;
Disadvantages: the degree of fit may be lower, the function may be too smooth, leading to underfitting.

